WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.950 --> 00:00:02.820
- [Narrator] This is what
the war in Ukraine looks like

00:00:02.820 --> 00:00:06.755
on one of China's most widely
followed state media accounts.

00:00:06.755 --> 00:00:07.588
(booming sounds)

00:00:07.588 --> 00:00:08.840
The Wall Street Journal analyzed

00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:10.780
nearly 70 Ukraine-related videos

00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:13.870
that were posted since the
start of the war in February.

00:00:13.870 --> 00:00:16.310
The account has also been
amplifying Russian narratives,

00:00:16.310 --> 00:00:18.450
like calling the conflict
a special operation

00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:20.205
and not an invasion.

00:00:20.205 --> 00:00:21.984
(presenter speaking in foreign language)

00:00:21.984 --> 00:00:23.201
(presenter speaking in foreign language)

00:00:23.201 --> 00:00:24.710
(soldiers speaking in foreign language)

00:00:24.710 --> 00:00:26.630
- [Narrator] These videos
also reminded followers

00:00:26.630 --> 00:00:28.634
to watch Russia's Victory Day parade

00:00:28.634 --> 00:00:31.780
and the Russian president
appeared five times more often

00:00:31.780 --> 00:00:33.710
than his Ukrainian counterpart.

00:00:33.710 --> 00:00:35.370
This pro-Russia perspective

00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:37.080
is just one message that's been echoed

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:38.450
on Chinese state media

00:00:38.450 --> 00:00:40.600
and getting shared with a wide audience.

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:41.433
- I do think that's kind

00:00:41.433 --> 00:00:43.270
of a propaganda opportunity,
or persuasion opportunity

00:00:43.270 --> 00:00:45.320
for the Chinese government.

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:47.730
- [Narrator] We examine how
closely Chinese state media

00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:48.830
is aligned with Russia,

00:00:48.830 --> 00:00:50.930
despite Beijing's
efforts to present itself

00:00:50.930 --> 00:00:53.143
as a neutral party to the war in Ukraine.

00:00:55.195 --> 00:00:56.920
(sirens ringing)

00:00:56.920 --> 00:00:59.680
From day one, China hasn't
outright condemned Russia

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:02.870
or taken part in Western
sanctions against Moscow.

00:01:02.870 --> 00:01:05.060
- China-Russia relationship
has been growing closer.

00:01:05.060 --> 00:01:07.430
- [Narrator] Maria Repnikova
is a political scientist,

00:01:07.430 --> 00:01:09.060
fluent in Russian and Chinese

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:11.180
and she's been monitoring
how Chinese state media

00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:13.520
has been reporting on
the Ukraine conflict.

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:15.070
(people clapping)

00:01:15.070 --> 00:01:17.110
She says their pact has gotten stronger

00:01:17.110 --> 00:01:18.730
as both countries' relationship

00:01:18.730 --> 00:01:20.930
with the US have gotten more contentious.

00:01:20.930 --> 00:01:22.290
- [Maria] So we see this
kind of increasing closeness

00:01:22.290 --> 00:01:24.020
of their ties as one fact

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:26.110
to the geopolitical relationship itself.

00:01:26.110 --> 00:01:28.100
- [Narrator] And examining
how the war has been covered

00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:30.720
on Chinese state media
reveals Beijing's tilt

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:33.180
towards Russia, like the
People's Daily account

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:35.350
on China's version of TikTok.

00:01:35.350 --> 00:01:38.080
In the nearly 70 videos
published on this account,

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:40.700
which has about 150 million followers,

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:42.370
none of them show destruction caused

00:01:42.370 --> 00:01:44.040
by Russian forces or describe

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:46.810
how civilians cope with
the ongoing fighting.

00:01:46.810 --> 00:01:49.740
14 of the posts are clips
from China's Foreign Ministry,

00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:52.110
which often repeats a
common line from Moscow

00:01:52.110 --> 00:01:53.727
with dramatic music.

00:01:53.727 --> 00:01:58.477
(presenter speaking in foreign language)

00:02:04.450 --> 00:02:06.280
- Some reports, the way they frame

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:08.320
or kind of explain pro-Russia stance

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:10.880
is often very much in this anti-US,

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:12.750
anti-NATO rhetoric.

00:02:12.750 --> 00:02:14.390
- [Narrator] There are 10 clips of Putin

00:02:14.390 --> 00:02:16.613
and they also echo a similar sentiment.

00:02:16.613 --> 00:02:21.030
(Putin speaking in foreign language)

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:23.720
- [Narrator] The decision
to single out sound bites

00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:25.280
like this show a deliberate attempt

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:26.330
from Chinese state media

00:02:26.330 --> 00:02:27.900
to paint the West as the villains

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:29.780
of the Ukraine crisis.

00:02:29.780 --> 00:02:31.250
Meanwhile, only two videos

00:02:31.250 --> 00:02:33.150
of Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelenskyy

00:02:33.150 --> 00:02:34.490
made it into the feed,

00:02:34.490 --> 00:02:36.480
and both clips show him
saying Western leaders

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:37.669
aren't doing enough.

00:02:37.669 --> 00:02:40.970
(Volodymyr Zelenskyy in foreign language)

00:02:40.970 --> 00:02:42.330
- [Narrator] People's Daily didn't respond

00:02:42.330 --> 00:02:43.813
to a request for comment.

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:46.900
Some Chinese state media organizations

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:48.500
have been getting specific directives

00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:50.150
on how to cover the war.

00:02:50.150 --> 00:02:51.600
Two days ahead of the invasion,

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:53.010
an online video platform

00:02:53.010 --> 00:02:56.460
that's part of a larger
state-backed publication posted this

00:02:56.460 --> 00:02:59.130
on Weibo, China's equivalent to Twitter.

00:02:59.130 --> 00:03:01.760
It appeared to be
instructions for its staff.

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:03.700
The memo said posts that are unfavorable

00:03:03.700 --> 00:03:07.080
to Russia or pro-West
should not be published.

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:09.220
The post was later removed from Weibo.

00:03:09.220 --> 00:03:11.100
The company and Horizon
News didn't respond

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:12.540
to a request for comment.

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:14.000
- This leak, what it's maybe showing us

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:15.390
is it kind of this attempt to maybe yeah,

00:03:15.390 --> 00:03:17.423
spin this war in a particular direction?

00:03:18.520 --> 00:03:19.780
- [Narrator] Journalists
in China are limited

00:03:19.780 --> 00:03:20.850
in what they can write about,

00:03:20.850 --> 00:03:21.740
which can be observed

00:03:21.740 --> 00:03:24.590
on the country's primary
international news network.

00:03:24.590 --> 00:03:27.930
In one news report posted
on CGTN's Facebook account,

00:03:27.930 --> 00:03:30.710
which has more than 118 million followers,

00:03:30.710 --> 00:03:32.430
two journalists documented their journey

00:03:32.430 --> 00:03:34.056
from Lviv to Kyiv.

00:03:34.056 --> 00:03:36.130
- Just one hour journey from Kyiv.

00:03:36.130 --> 00:03:38.210
- [Maria] They're not allowed
to be maybe too sympathetic

00:03:38.210 --> 00:03:40.111
or too pro-Western or too pro-Ukraine.

00:03:40.111 --> 00:03:42.930
- We definitely see more debris, ruins

00:03:42.930 --> 00:03:46.230
after brought by the conflict and crisis.

00:03:46.230 --> 00:03:47.700
- [Maria] And they have to
kind of say it's happening

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:49.440
but they're not able
to provide the context

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:51.240
or maybe include more footage

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:54.040
of various victims that
would provide the context.

00:03:54.040 --> 00:03:57.003
- [Narrator] CGTN did not
respond to a request for comment.

00:03:58.450 --> 00:04:00.010
Much of that context was missing

00:04:00.010 --> 00:04:02.140
from Chinese state media when mass graves

00:04:02.140 --> 00:04:04.740
were discovered in the
Ukrainian town of Bucha.

00:04:04.740 --> 00:04:06.880
The incident triggered global condemnation

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:09.070
with many countries calling
for an investigation

00:04:09.070 --> 00:04:11.980
into alleged atrocities
committed by Russia.

00:04:11.980 --> 00:04:15.550
- There's nothing less
happening than major war crimes.

00:04:15.550 --> 00:04:18.670
Responsible nations have to come together

00:04:18.670 --> 00:04:20.940
to hold these perpetrators accountable.

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:22.350
- [Narrator] But the
story was largely ignored

00:04:22.350 --> 00:04:24.020
by most Chinese media outlets

00:04:24.020 --> 00:04:26.260
while the Kremlin called
videos from Bucha fake

00:04:26.260 --> 00:04:29.100
without presenting any
evidence to back the claim.

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:31.500
Beijing described the images
of the civilian deaths

00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:33.240
as deeply disturbing but warned

00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:36.447
against making quote, unquote,
"unfounded accusations."

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:38.720
As the war stretches on,

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:41.110
choosing not to condemn
Russia for its actions,

00:04:41.110 --> 00:04:44.030
like in Bucha, can put
Beijing in a tricky position.

00:04:44.030 --> 00:04:45.130
- China still very much cares

00:04:45.130 --> 00:04:46.350
about its relation with the West,

00:04:46.350 --> 00:04:47.620
especially for economic reasons

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:49.000
but I think it's very important

00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:51.470
to not necessarily ruining
this competitive edge

00:04:51.470 --> 00:04:53.280
by over-aligning with Russia.

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:54.950
- [Narrator] So Beijing
has officially maintained

00:04:54.950 --> 00:04:56.960
a neutral stance amid the war

00:04:56.960 --> 00:04:58.380
and this diplomatic position

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:01.310
hasn't contradicted what's
been published on state media.

00:05:01.310 --> 00:05:02.570
- It's definitely using
it as an opportunity

00:05:02.570 --> 00:05:05.260
to assert its stance and its rise

00:05:05.260 --> 00:05:07.220
as distinct from and arguably,

00:05:07.220 --> 00:05:08.730
also counter to the West,

00:05:08.730 --> 00:05:10.190
questioning the world, the US,

00:05:10.190 --> 00:05:11.930
critiquing the US.

00:05:11.930 --> 00:05:13.010
- [Narrator] So as long as the US

00:05:13.010 --> 00:05:15.080
is portrayed as a global troublemaker,

00:05:15.080 --> 00:05:17.040
the war in Ukraine remains a useful tool

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:19.190
for Russia to justify its invasion

00:05:19.190 --> 00:05:20.720
and China to boost its image

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:23.218
to audiences at home and abroad.

00:05:23.218 --> 00:05:25.829
(dramatic music)

